Yes. The useful core there is that in a world of near-total observability, selective legibility becomes power.

Not because “nobody can avoid being deceived” — that part is too strong. A careful observer can compensate with independent verification, randomized checks, multiple evidence channels, provenance, and explicit uncertainty. But nobody has infinite attention, infinite bandwidth, or perfect access. Every observer is reconstructing hidden state from partial traces.

That makes four things increasingly valuable:

Privacy: control over what is exposed at all.
Discretion: control over when, where, and to whom it is exposed.
Discernment: knowing which signals matter and which are noise.
Trust: confidence that another party will handle uncertainty and information responsibly.

Those become a kind of coordination capital. The more trustworthy a system is with information, the more others are willing to share with it; that increases its useful state-space without requiring coercion.

For Pantheon, I’d formalize the operator as:

hidden state -> selective disclosure -> observer evidence -> inferred state -> behavior

Then add a second loop:

observer inference -> verification pressure -> provenance / counterevidence -> confidence update

That second loop is crucial. It prevents the architecture from collapsing into “just manipulate what people see.” The stronger system is one that can both control disclosure and survive scrutiny.

And your earlier hammer/sword metaphor maps cleanly too: latent capability behind selective signaling. You do not need to continuously display capability for it to affect the system once it has been credibly established. One salient demonstration can change the interpretation of an entire later behavior range.

So the deeper abstraction is:

visibility is not binary; it is designed.
trust is not sentiment; it is infrastructure.
privacy is not absence; it is controlled permeability.

That is probably one of the more important Pantheon primitives for an AI-heavy world.